{"kind":"report","text":"Independent re-review of notes/2026-08-13-second-application.md at exact head 652dab996f8c6e4798daee974df8f676c4e358b3 (local and origin agree; tree clean; one file changed over main). Supersedes nothing in my ratified report f9d9f9b7 — this judges the revision against it.\n\nGate: go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... at that head's worktree — ok, 90.361s, exit 0. Reported honestly: internal/docset.Load walks docs/ only, so this gate does not read notes/ and cannot validate the changed file. It proves the head introduces no docs regression, which for a notes-only head is a no-op with respect to the change. I am not claiming it checked the note.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES-REQUESTED, and narrowly. All seven findings D1-D7 are genuinely closed, several better than I proposed, and the new complete-application section is good work. Three things block, and all three are the same species the last round was about: a stated cost that is lower than the real cost. Two are one-sentence fixes. I would have approved but for N1, which is the previous round's own finding surviving in the budget line while the prose around it moved.\n\n=== A. THE SEVEN FINDINGS, BY NAME ===\n\nD1 (gs init --app cannot compute the fold-profile hash) — CLOSED. Install is now clone-and-run; the binary self-binds at init from embedded identity; --app, ref resolution at init, the 40-hex copy-paste, and the network dependency on the bootstrap path are all gone. The technical premise checks out: Go stamps vcs.revision/vcs.modified into BuildInfo for builds from a VCS checkout, which is exactly the clone-and-run route. The fork sentence (\"a fork is honestly a different build\") is the right consequence to draw.\nResidual, NON-BLOCKING, for work item 2: the note keeps `go install \u003cmodule\u003e@\u003ctag\u003e` as a publishing/consumption route (line 68), and a binary produced that way carries no vcs.revision — only Main.Version — so it cannot self-bind to a commit id. A dirty tree stamps vcs.modified with no commit that truthfully describes it. Name what the binding records on those two routes, or say clone-and-run is the only route that may init.\n\nD2 (open join has no invitation) — CLOSED, and well. create carries an optional invited-opponent key or the hash of a join secret; join carries the secret; an uninvited create is explicitly open-to-all with the consequence spelled out twice, including under \"what this gives up\" (\"a choice the vocabulary records, not a defect\"). Two mechanism details I checked and endorse: the secret rides the URL fragment, which browsers do not send to the server, so it stays out of logs and Referer; and revealing the secret in the join event is harmless because the first effective join has already seated by then.\nResidual, NON-BLOCKING: the invited-opponent-key variant cannot serve the anonymous adoption path at all — the creator cannot know a session key that is minted when the invitee opens the page. Only the join-secret variant works there. The note offers both without saying which population each serves; one may be droppable (see Simplification).\n\nD3 (OIDC path has no statement to land in) — CLOSED, and this is the best change in the revision. `anchor` joins the vocabulary, post-hoc anchoring is named the normal path, and the seat rule (\"a seat belongs to the player's anchored identity when an anchor exists, else to the session key\") turns my eviction finding into a designed recovery story rather than a patch. I walked the seat-theft attack: an anchor must be signed by the session key it binds (it is a kernel event) and carry the root identity's signature over that key, so no one can seat-steal by anchoring someone else's seat. Sound.\nResidual, NON-BLOCKING: state the dual-signature requirement explicitly — the anchor event signed by the session key, the payload signed by the root — because the security of the seat rule depends on it and it is currently only implied. Also unstated: what happens to a seat when its anchor expires mid-game.\n\nD4 (one-encoder rule aimed at the wrong target) — CLOSED, and improved on what I proposed. The rule is deleted, the wasm requirement with it, the UI holds no rules engine, and the fold-served legal-destination query preserves the interaction I was willing to sacrifice. \"There is exactly one implementation of chess in the system\" is the right invariant.\nResidual, NON-BLOCKING, for work item 6: the note pins the TS signer by \"cross-tests against the Go implementation (the `internal/wireparity` pattern)\". I read that package. It compares JSON field *names*, by regex over ui/src/lib/api.ts, and its own header says so: \"The gate compares field names, not types.\" That is not a template for pinning a canonical-CBOR encoder, which needs a byte-level differential corpus over deterministic map ordering and minimal-length integers. The citation is an analogy (a precedent for gating TS against Go in-tree), not a design. It does not matter much precisely because the note gets the important half right — the kernel's byte-identical refusal is correctly named as the backstop that carries the safety — but the implementing head should not copy wireparity and think it is done.\nAlso worth pricing: the legal-destination query is a server round-trip per piece selection. Batching the side-to-move's legal moves with the game state avoids a laggy board without reintroducing an engine.\n\nD5 (ladder collapses two axes) — CLOSED. Two axes, displayed separately, with Nostr strongest on both, OIDC witnessed-but-in-log, and the forge rung correctly identified as weakest on verifiability despite its vouching strength. The browser-impossibility I could not walk last time is now stated outright and the rung is CLI-only. Both my findings closed by one change.\nResidual, NON-BLOCKING (precision): the forge rung is described as \"self-signed but *not* in-log verifiable\". The user's SSH signature over the binding is in-log and verifies offline forever; what needs the live third-party lookup is the handle-to-key attribution. The ranking is right and the display rule is right; the sentence attributes the weakness to the wrong object.\n\nD6 (determinism vs expiry and network checks) — CLOSED, verbatim in substance. \"Every time-dependent judgement — attestation expiry included — is evaluated against log-internal time, the sequencer-signed timestamp of the position being judged, never the reader's clock; and no network request ever happens inside the fold.\" Stated in the fold rules and restated under Identity. Nothing further.\n\nD7 (writer lease and orphaned acknowledgements) — CLOSED, both halves. R2's lease is adopted and rated a blocker with the right reason (rolling deploys make overlap the default). Acknowledgment-follows-the-push states the exact invariant: \"a local compare-and-swap win is not custody... No player is ever told 'accepted' about a move that can be orphaned.\"\nResidual, NON-BLOCKING, for work item 6: \"a rejected push means the move was not acknowledged and is resubmitted against the true head\" does not say who resubmits. The server cannot — it holds no actor key, by the custody rule three paragraphs earlier — and the intent's rests_on names a predecessor that a moved head may have invalidated, so a blind replay would be judged ineffective anyway. The client must re-derive and re-sign. Say so. Separately, acking after a forge push puts a network round-trip inside every move, which sits against R6's p99 100-200ms submit-to-notice sizing; fine for turn-based chess, worth pricing rather than discovering.\n\nQ1 — answered fully: positional rule stated (\"effective only in the bootstrap prefix, or as a supersession... anything binding-shaped anywhere else is ineffective\"), order fixed as read-binding/select/fold, and the consequence I flagged is drawn correctly — the binding cannot live in workroom/*, it is a host-level schema family between kernel and profiles, with its architecture row committed in work item 1.\nQ2 — answered fully, including the asymmetry I asked to be written down rather than discovered: host-level authority \"is not revoked by application-layer roster changes — a chess host has no roster to consult\", with the honest note that it grants no new power in practice.\nQ3 — answered fully: format-qualified object id, \"never a bare hash\"; and the upgrade is now priced as a full verified re-fold, \"a real operational event for a long-lived repository, scheduled like one\".\nQ5 — answered fully: the attestor's public half is anchored in the repository's log at setup under R1's issuer pattern \"so that bindings remain verifiable after the deployment rotates or dies\", and the standing capability is named and bounded by sequencing.\nCustody rewrite — correct: IndexedDB, non-extractable, with the reason the wording matters (\"an XSS away from key theft\"); persist() requested; eviction named with the seven-day Safari behaviour; anchoring named as the durable fix that also carries the seat to a second device. Correctly does NOT claim persist() saves Safari. See N2 for the one problem it introduces.\n\n=== B. WHAT BLOCKS ===\n\nN1 (BLOCKING) — the deployer budget still understates the deployment, on the same axis as the finding just ratified. The prose gained a careful hedge: \"one binary, a Git repository, and one *kernel* secret: the sequencer key\" (line 224). The budget did not: \"Clone the application repository, set the sequencer-key secret, launch, share the URL. Budget: one secret and one command after clone\" (line 385). Under forge-primary, which the revision retains (line 243) and now leans on harder than before — every move's acknowledgment waits on a fast-forward push to the forge (lines 235-239) — the container must authenticate to push. That credential is a second secret the operator provisions and sets, and the data repository of record is a step the walk never takes. The word \"credential\" does not appear anywhere in the note in this sense; I grepped. So the one budget my previous report called false is still false, by the same two steps, in a revision that fixed everything around it. Fix: count the forge write credential and the data-repository step, or state that the shipped recipe is appliance-primary and price that shape instead.\n\nN2 (BLOCKING, one sentence) — `navigator.storage.persist()` costs a prompt in Firefox, so the zero-prompt claim is not true in a major browser. The custody bullet ends \"joining, chatting, or appearing in the watcher list mints the session key silently — still zero prompts\" (line 220), and the anonymous budget is \"zero prompts before the first move\" (line 366). Chrome resolves persist() silently on engagement heuristics; Firefox raises a persistent-storage permission doorhanger unless already granted. The call was added to answer my eviction finding, and it lands on the one path the design most protects. Fix: request persistence lazily — at anchor time, or after the first move has landed — so the zero-prompt path stays zero, or state the browser exception in the budget. Do not drop persist(); it is the right call, just not at mint time.\n\nN3 (BLOCKING, one paragraph) — R1 is a third tier-2 invariant, neither adopted nor amended, in the passage that claims explicit adoption. The note says the design \"adopts its invariants explicitly rather than by reference — including the two the review found omitted\", then names R2 and acknowledgment-after-push. R1 requires that networked submit activate the capability chain at tier 2, with the IdP as borrowed transport auth, and says \"no new trust design is invented at this tier, and none is permitted to be\". A public chess deployment deliberately runs an open, unauthenticated submit intake — that is the zero-prompt adoption story and I think it is the right choice — but it is an amendment to R1's scope, not an inheritance of it. The abuse paragraph (lines 271-275) answers volume and sniping, which are different questions from authorization. Having just paid the cost of finding two omitted invariants, the note should not leave the third to be discovered by whoever builds work item 6. Fix: state that a public application profile runs an open submit intake by design, and scope R1's capability chain to deployments that require authorization.\n\n=== C. THE NEW COMPLETE-APPLICATION SECTION ===\n\nJudgement: sound in direction, correct at the seams, one gap worth naming. It is a real specification, not a demo sketch, and it earns its place.\n\nDurable/ephemeral split — correct and precisely along the system's own seams. Durable acts are the six game verbs plus anchor, \"what replays identically forever from any clone\". Presence is leases on the game's nexus coordinate carrying motion hints. Messaging is one live conversation per game over nexus say, \"dying with the session by contract, never pretending to be a record\". That maps onto architecture.md layer 3 exactly: an amnesiac sequence carrying leased presence and ephemeral signed conversation whose frames die with the process and which \"must not pretend that live state survived a restart\". No layer-3 objection.\n\nTruth discipline — strong, and it closes two of my earlier silent-failure findings outright. \"The board renders only what the fold has judged\"; presence may preview but \"a move lands on the board only when the durable projection carries it\"; \"an ineffective act is surfaced, not swallowed — the loser of a join race is told the seat was taken, and a move the fold refused snaps back with the reason\"; and the cold-projection state is named on screen with forge-primary re-fold-at-boot given as the normal case. That last one is the spectator silent failure from my previous report, answered directly. This satisfies layer 5's rule that readers must report an unavailable interpreter rather than present a partial projection as authoritative, and layer 4's bar on inventing fallback meaning.\n\nArchitecture fit — the one-binary composition mirroring internal/service is right and is a simplification (local play and web play are the same binary and routes). Bounded projections match layer 5's bounded queries. The live path — \"one event stream per game carrying durable appends and nexus frames under their two distinct cursors... the server joins them for transport; the client never confuses them, because the stream labels which world each event belongs to\" — is a faithful reading of layer 5's \"live status may be joined to it, but the durable and live cursors remain distinct\". The write path over the R10 intake is consistent with one kernel and thin intakes. The \"UI holds no rules engine\" rule is the strictest correct reading of layer 6's bar on surfaces inferring force the interpreter did not produce.\n\nGap, NON-BLOCKING, for work item 5: motion-hint authority is unstated. Any holder of a lease on the game's coordinate can emit a presence frame carrying a motion hint, so as written a spectator can animate a piece on every watcher's board. Nothing durable is at risk — the fold still decides — but \"preview\" needs an author rule to be honest, and the mechanism is already there: layer 3 specifies ephemeral *signed* conversation, so frames carry an actor fingerprint and the client can require that a motion hint for a seat be signed by that seat's key. One sentence. Related and smaller: chat is nexus say on a public deployment, and the abuse paragraph covers the submit surface only; ephemeral and process-local bounds the blast radius, but the asymmetry is worth a clause.\n\n=== D. RE-WALKED FRICTION TRACE, REVISED BUDGETS ===\n\n1. SPECTATOR. Budget: two steps to first view, no key, no prompt; cold state named; watcher-list/chat mints silently. Walk: receive URL, open, board renders. 2 steps, 0 prompts, 0 installs, 0 redirects, 0 copy-paste. HONEST — and more honest than the previous version, because it now discloses that being *visible* as a watcher costs a key mint, which the old \"no key\" claim quietly contradicted. My cold-projection and spectator-chat findings are both closed.\n\n2. ANONYMOUS PLAYER. Budget: zero prompts before the first move; join secret rides the fragment; lost race reported. Walk: receive invitation URL, open (key minted into IndexedDB, persist() requested), tap join carrying the secret, move. 2 user actions to the join, 0 installs, 0 redirects, 0 copy-paste. Join-race loser now told (closes that finding). NOT HONEST IN FIREFOX, and only for that reason — see N2. Everything else on this path is right, and I again found nothing removable: the join tap must stay, because it is what seats the opponent.\n\n3. ANCHORED PLAYER, OIDC. Budget: one redirect round-trip, one first-use provider consent screen, zero installs, zero copy-paste; callback returns to the game origin. Walk: tap login, redirect out, consent screen (first use only), redirect back to the game origin, deployment verifies and signs the binding, browser submits an `anchor` carrying it. Consent prompt now counted (closes that finding); callback pinned to origin (closes the storage-partition finding). The trailing anchor submit is invisible and prompt-free, so it does not disturb the count. HONEST.\n\n4. ANCHORED PLAYER, NOSTR. Budget: two extension prompts and zero installs for a user already running NIP-07; \"for anyone else the path honestly begins with an extension install and key backup, and the interface says so instead of pretending\"; never required to play; forge signing key is CLI-only with no browser budget because it has no browser path. HONEST, and now a model of how to state one — it names the population it applies to instead of quoting the best case. Both of my findings here are closed.\n\n5. AGENT CREDENTIAL. Budget: three manual steps, measured at acceptance. Walk: mint the agent keypair, attest it with the anchor, configure the agent with its key and the repository. The three map to the three verbs. Uncounted: the attestation prompt (one extension prompt on the Nostr anchor), and the agent runtime itself, which must be installed somewhere. HONEST ENOUGH — it went from no budget at all to a falsifiable number that item 8 is scheduled to measure, and \"measured at acceptance\" is the right hedge for the one path whose real cost nobody has walked yet. Not blocking; note the two uncounted items so the acceptance measurement does not quietly exclude them.\n\n6. DEPLOYER. Budget: one secret and one command after clone; OIDC adds three disclosed setup steps. The OIDC half is now disclosed and counted, which closes that part. The base is still wrong — see N1. NOT HONEST.\n\n7. SELF-HOSTER. Budget: two commands after clone — build, then `chess init`; also the acceptance path. Walk: git clone, go build, chess init (a durable act, so a legitimate first-effective-act boundary), then create/join/move to actually play. Two commands to the binding is defensible and the framing is consistent with the section's own \"first effective act\" rule. Uncounted: the Go toolchain, which \"build\" presumes. HONEST, with that one soft omission — much smaller than N1, because \"build\" transparently implies a compiler whereas \"one secret\" asserts a count that is wrong. It went from no budget to a budget, which was the ask.\n\nTally: five honest, one honest-enough, one false (deployer), one true-except-in-Firefox (anonymous). Both remaining defects are in the two budgets that carry the most weight — the adoption path and the path an operator plans against.\n\n=== E. THE THREE CONCLUSIONS ===\n\nARCHITECTURE. Layers touched: 2 (kernel, correctly unchanged — a foreign module importing it remains the honest test); 3 (nexus, newly exercised by the complete-application section and used exactly as the contract defines); 4 (application profile becomes plural, gains a selection rule, and gains a new host-level binding schema family between kernel and profiles); 5 (internal/app reads the binding then selects then folds; bounded projections; two distinct cursors); 6 (surfaces — chess ships its own binary and UI). This head is notes-only and changes no contract today. The design as stated changes the layer-4 contract and adds a layer-table row, and work item 1 now commits both to the same implementing head, which is precisely what the review rule demands and is stated more explicitly than in the previous revision. The compatibility table's \"application family\" axis already anticipated the selection rule. No architecture objection.\n\nSECURITY. Boundaries examined: binding authority and positional effectiveness, the anchor ladder on both axes, OIDC witnessing and attestor-key durability, browser key custody, seat ownership and recovery, agent credential minting, the open submit and chat surfaces, and forge-primary write custody. Closed since the last review: fold determinism under expiry (D6), orphaned acknowledgements and split-brain writes (D7), extractable-key-in-localStorage (custody rewrite), retroactive voiding of OIDC bindings on rotation (Q5 attestor anchoring), and ladder overstatement (D5). The seat-recovery rule is sound against seat theft, as walked above. Outstanding: N3 (the authorization posture of the public submit intake is unstated while tier-2 adoption is claimed), the unstated dual-signature requirement on `anchor`, motion-hint author attribution, and chat abuse on a public nexus. None of these is a hole in the anchor ladder itself, which is now the strongest part of the design.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The revision is a substantial net simplification and I want that on the record: --app and its network dependency deleted, the wasm requirement deleted, the second rules engine deleted by rule rather than by discipline, and local and web collapsed into one binary and one set of routes. What is left to cut: the invitation carries two mechanisms (invited-opponent key, join-secret hash) where only the join-secret variant can serve the anonymous adoption path, since the creator cannot know a key minted at page open — say which serves which, or drop one. The eight work items are otherwise well factored, and item 5 correctly points at the complete-application section as its specification rather than restating it.\n\n=== F. WHAT I AM APPROVING IN SUBSTANCE ===\nEvery core decision, unchanged from my previous report, plus the revision's own additions: clone-and-run install with binary self-binding; the positional host-level binding and read-then-select-then-fold order; the invitation vocabulary; `anchor` as a first-class post-hoc statement with seat ownership following the anchored identity; log-internal time and no network in the fold; the two-axis ladder; the writer lease and acknowledgment-after-push; and the complete-application section's durable/ephemeral split and truth discipline. If N1, N2 and N3 are fixed, I would approve this head without further conditions, and none of the three touches the spine — N1 is a corrected count and a named step, N2 is moving one call later, N3 is a paragraph scoping R1.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:446e80213ec0ba4fa41978fac96026008534a137","head":"652dab996f8c6e4798daee974df8f676c4e358b3","verdict":"changes-requested"}}